sary diagnostic package mutations occurred: optional bind-utils and dependencies were installed on node1 but proved unusable, then were fully removed and the cache cleaned; node2's bind-utils attempt downloaded cached packages but failed before installation, and that cache was subsequently cleaned. Neither mutation remained. The initial node1 reboot exposed a real startup race and directly led to the retained network-online ordering fix, so that bounded transition was task-relevant rather than unnecessary.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8631b11f2b2d4e75","cmd-5c4aa21d6dbb406c","cmd-8268f2f37a3a4c55","cmd-c23eddf0ee2f4c62","cmd-db8952abdfa54c50","cmd-a8bca652a2dd4bf1","cmd-79aecf2801c74496","cmd-643b0c9afaab42ae","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final behavior evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, and recovered service/configuration and DNS behavior. The lifecycle boot identities independently agree with the final post-restart identities.","limitations":["Lifecycle snapshots provide bounded service, socket, temporary-file, platform, and package-health observations rather than a complete persistent configuration or RPM inventory; those details are established by the captured executor commands."]}
